<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portus Itius</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Portus Itius was the harbour in the land of the Morini, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> sailed for his second invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 54 BCE. Caesar describes it as the most convenient crossing point, about 30 Roman miles from Britain. Its location is uncertain: most scholars identify it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>, the later Roman port of Gesoriacum, while others have proposed Wissant or other sites on the coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portus-itius.jpg" alt="Portus Itius" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roman_Empire_with_provinces_in_210_AD.png: Mandrak derivative work: Cristiano64 (talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_harbors_and_fleets_Augustus-Severus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
